\section{Variance components}{

The error variance of a PV-based estimate has two sources: sampling
(which students were sampled) and measurement (each student's
proficiency is uncertain, which is represented by drawing M PVs). The
returned columns are:\tabular{lll}{
   column \tab symbol \tab definition \cr
   \code{samplingVar} \tab U \tab mean over PVs of the replicate sampling variance \cr
   \code{imputationVar} \tab B \tab variance of the M full-sample PV estimates, \code{var(theta_m)} (divisor M - 1), \emph{before} Rubin's correction \cr
   \code{se} \tab  \tab \code{sqrt(U + (1 + 1/M) * B)} \cr
}


\strong{Why (1 + 1/M) B rather than B.} B estimates how much the statistic
varies from one PV to another. The reported estimate, however, is the
\emph{average} of only M PV estimates, not the value that would be obtained
with infinitely many PVs. That average has its own simulation error,
with variance B / M, which must be added to the total. Hence the
measurement component is B + B/M = (1 + 1/M) B (Rubin, 1987). Put
another way, U + B follows from the law of total variance; the
additional B/M term corrects for using a finite number of PVs. The
correction matters most when M is small: it adds 20\% to B with 5 PVs
and 10\% with 10 PVs.

\strong{Recovering \code{se} from the columns.}

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode">}}\preformatted{res <- pvMean(...)
all.equal(res$se,
          sqrt(res$samplingVar + (1 + 1 / res$nPVs) * res$imputationVar))
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

Computing \code{sqrt(samplingVar + imputationVar)} omits the B/M term and so
slightly understates the standard error. The share of error variance
due to measurement is \code{(1 + 1/M) * B / se^2}.

If \code{repWeights} is \code{NULL}, \code{samplingVar} and therefore \code{se} are \code{NA};
\code{imputationVar} is still returned.

Reference: Rubin, D. B. (1987). \emph{Multiple Imputation for Nonresponse in
Surveys}. Wiley.
}

